\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Reduction without normalized averaging}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{Since #{3\mid 24}, there is no normalized Reynolds
average #{24^{-1}\sum_{g\in 2T}g} over #{\mathbb F_3}.
The two-column decomposition and the specific form in
\ref{fdip-00V8} are explicit matrix identities; neither uses
that average. The perfect integral form and the determinant
square in \ref{fdip-00V6} therefore survive this reduction.}

\p{In characteristic #{2}, use the integral basis
#{(1,\qi,\qj,h)} and the Gram matrix in
\ref{fdip-00V3}. The elements
#{1,\qi,\qj,\qk} are not a basis there: the integral
identity #{\qk=2h-1-\qi -\qj} loses its #{h} coefficient
modulo #{2}. Neither the half-integral norm pairing nor the
modulo-#{3} trace formula defines the form in that
characteristic. Its integral matrix and the degree argument
of \ref{fdip-00V6} do.}
